WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.510 --> 00:00:01.480
My name is Dan Korsun.

00:00:01.480 --> 00:00:03.480
I'm studying physics here
at MIT and my research

00:00:03.480 --> 00:00:05.970
is into the effects of
radiation damage on ReBCO

00:00:05.970 --> 00:00:07.740
high temperature
superconductors.

00:00:07.740 --> 00:00:10.415
So ReBCO stands for rare-earth
barium-copper oxide.

00:00:10.415 --> 00:00:11.790
And it's a type
of superconductor

00:00:11.790 --> 00:00:13.165
that we're looking
at here at MIT

00:00:13.165 --> 00:00:15.540
to build the next generation
of fusion devices.

00:00:15.540 --> 00:00:18.020
So nuclear fusion is kind of
like making a star on earth.

00:00:18.020 --> 00:00:20.130
It's replicating what
the sun and other stars

00:00:20.130 --> 00:00:23.550
do to produce almost limitless
amounts of clean, carbon

00:00:23.550 --> 00:00:24.353
free energy.

00:00:24.353 --> 00:00:25.770
And so here at
MIT, we're actually

00:00:25.770 --> 00:00:27.270
building a new
device called SPARC.

00:00:27.270 --> 00:00:29.280
And SPARC will be the
first fusion device

00:00:29.280 --> 00:00:32.393
to actually produce more
power than it takes to run.

00:00:32.393 --> 00:00:33.810
And SPARC will do
this by actually

00:00:33.810 --> 00:00:35.580
using magnets for
the first time made

00:00:35.580 --> 00:00:37.150
of this material called ReBCO.

00:00:37.150 --> 00:00:38.968
And ReBCO is a very,
very special material.

00:00:38.968 --> 00:00:41.010
So it can actually-- it's
a superconductor, which

00:00:41.010 --> 00:00:42.835
means it can carry
incredibly high amounts

00:00:42.835 --> 00:00:44.710
of currents and under
specialized conditions.

00:00:44.710 --> 00:00:46.880
So here we actually have
a traditional copper cable

00:00:46.880 --> 00:00:49.462
that you would see a
traditional fusion device.

00:00:49.462 --> 00:00:51.170
And here we have a
little piece of ReBCO.

00:00:51.170 --> 00:00:53.490
And under certain conditions,
this little piece of ReBCO

00:00:53.490 --> 00:00:55.365
can actually carry the
same amount of current

00:00:55.365 --> 00:00:56.910
as this big copper cable.

00:00:56.910 --> 00:00:58.550
And that is what
enables us in SPARC,

00:00:58.550 --> 00:01:00.675
and in the future, such
devices to actually produce

00:01:00.675 --> 00:01:02.580
such large amounts of power.

00:01:02.580 --> 00:01:04.019
And so what my
research focuses on

00:01:04.019 --> 00:01:07.320
is emulating how the neutrons
produced in fusion reactions

00:01:07.320 --> 00:01:08.910
will actually damage
ReBCO and impact

00:01:08.910 --> 00:01:10.202
its performance over time.

00:01:10.202 --> 00:01:11.910
And so very little is
actually understood

00:01:11.910 --> 00:01:14.520
about how ReBCO will be
damaged by such neutrons.

00:01:14.520 --> 00:01:16.850
And my work is actually
focused on applying reactor

00:01:16.850 --> 00:01:19.140
relevant irradiation conditions
like low temperatures

00:01:19.140 --> 00:01:21.450
and high fields to
these ReBCO samples

00:01:21.450 --> 00:01:24.510
to emulate and predict how
such magnets will perform over

00:01:24.510 --> 00:01:26.160
time in fusion
devices, which will

00:01:26.160 --> 00:01:29.660
impact the viability of
these devices going forward.

